    "answer": {
      "short": "$k>0$ and $h<0$, or $k<0$ and $h>0$: equivalently, $kh<0$. The conditions are unchanged for $y=k(a^{-x})+h$.",
      "worked": "At an $x$-intercept, $y=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}k(a^x)+h&=0\\\\a^x&=-\\frac{h}{k}\\end{align*}$$\nSince $a^x$ takes every positive value, an intercept exists exactly when $-\\frac{h}{k}>0$. Thus $k$ and $h$ must have opposite signs: $k>0$, $h<0$, or $k<0$, $h>0$.\n\nFor the negative exponent,\n$$\\begin{align*}k(a^{-x})+h&=0\\\\a^{-x}&=-\\frac{h}{k}\\end{align*}$$\nThe expression $a^{-x}$ also takes every positive value, so the conditions are unchanged.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 86
